== F-20: stopped, a defect rather than a missing test ==
Research before writing any test, because a test that pins unsafe behaviour as correct is worse than no test.
Path 1 (cpic_engine.select_annotation, "candidates = matched or usable"). A constructed probe, not an observed
event: the real CYP2C19 Poor Metabolizer report fixture, with clopidogrel's guideline left holding only CPIC's real
Normal Metabolizer row (classification Strong, wording from test-data/reference/cpic_expectations.json). The real
report, untouched, gives Ineffective with "Avoid clopidogrel if possible...". The probe gives "No PGx-guided
adjustment identified", action "If considering clopidogrel, use at standard dose (75 mg/day)", label rule
standard_dosing. The only safeguard was the warning "showing the strongest available row. Treat with extra
caution."; the request-time contradiction check did not degrade it. "Strongest" ranks evidence strength, not
caution, so when the fallback fires on an asserted phenotype it can serve the reassuring row of a different
phenotype - the false-reassurance direction.
Whether it happens on real data: over the 400 cached cohort reports x 6 drugs (2,400 evaluations) the fallback fires
309 times (fluorouracil 98, simvastatin 209, clopidogrel 2), and evaluate() returns Unknown in all 309, because in
each the phenotype was not asserted and the earlier gate returned before the fallback could produce a label. S1-S6
and the A4 rows never reached it (Part A). So it is latent, not observed: on real data the protection comes from a
different layer, not from the fallback. A CPIC or PharmCAT wording change that leaves an asserted phenotype with no
matching row would reach it.
Path 2 (an Unknown result carrying a CPIC directive in action): NOT MEASURED. Reading the code, two engine
sub-paths label Unknown while still building the recommendation from the CPIC row (the fallback_unmatched rule and
the request-time contradiction downgrade), and main.py rebuilds without CPIC text only in the coverage-gate case.
The measurement that would show whether either occurs was stopped before it ran; no result exists.
No F-20 test or engine change is committed. Fixing path 1 changes backend/app/cpic_engine.py, which is in the
scientific core, so it needs its own plan, zero-regression and a re-bind, and the owner's decision.

== F-19, the remaining four items (owner: "continue", 2026-11-07, after F-20 went live at e68e56f) ==
Criteria, written before any change. Each claim is replaced with what an artifact on disk says, and each gets a guard
so it cannot go stale silently again. Docs and tests only: nothing under backend/app or app/lib, so no re-bind.
D1  Latency. README.md:202 ("~52 s - p50 51.27 s, p95 53.56 s (n=20)", "~2.6 s", "~20x") and docs/DEMO_SCRIPT.md:19
    are stale in all three numbers, not only the live one. Replaced from one same-day, same-input measurement:
    F-20's six demo files, run locally (audit/phase9/partb/F20/s1-s6/requests.jsonl) and live
    (audit/phase9/partb/F20/live-after-s1s6/requests.jsonl), 2026-11-07: local median 1.37 s, live median 64.61 s,
    live range 60.8-65.5 s, median live/local ratio 46.7x. Guard: a test re-derives both medians from those two
    files and requires README and DEMO_SCRIPT to quote them, and requires 51.27 and 53.56 not to be stated as fact.
D2  Concordance. reports/validation_report.md contradicts itself: its table calls CYP2C9 an "exact match" and its
    summary says "2 of 2 exact", while its own note further down (and PROJECT_STATUS.md:1090) says CYP2C9 is a
    conservative refusal, Undetermined where GeT-RM says *1/*2. The table row and the summary are aligned with that
    correction; CYP2C19 is left as it stands. No new factual claim is introduced. Guard: "2 of 2 exact" joins the
    existing spelling list, and a regex catches any CYP2C9 line that calls it an exact match.
D3  Placeholder. .github/workflows/build-apk.yml:29 names https://YOURNAME-pharmaguard.hf.space as API_BASE_URL; the
    backend is https://pharmaguard-api-baml.onrender.com, as deploy-web.yml already documents. Comment only; the
    workflow runs on version tags and manual dispatch, so a push to main does not trigger it. Guard: no workflow
    names YOURNAME.
D4  Provenance. README.md:516 says "every clinical statement is provenance-verified to a CPIC source". main.py's own
    comment says served wording may be the deterministic template, "which no provenance check reads, so nothing here
    may claim every statement was verified". The bullet is rewritten to say what the served disclosure says. Guard:
    README must not assert that every clinical statement is provenance-verified.
S   Sabotage, each restored byte-identical: put each stale claim back, and quote a wrong median, and its guard must
    turn red.
C   The full backend suite passes.
